%S A398590 0,1,2,5,7,11,14,16,19,25,28,38,41,42,44,57,62,73,80,82,87,99,107,108,
%T A398590 120,125,129,141,154,164,181,182,187,197,197,216,234,240,237,269,279,
%U A398590 298,305,309,313,345,350,362,375,385,391,432,440,442,449,456,474,490,504,537
%N A398590 Number of distinct entries k of the n X n multiplication table such that k + n + 1 + floor((k-1)/n) is an entry of the (n+1) X (n+1) multiplication table.
%C A398590 The sequence counts distinct products whose row-column positions correspond under (q, r) -> (q+1, r) in the row-wise arrangements of two consecutive multiplication tables.
%C A398590 Write k = q*n + r uniquely with 0 <= q < n and 1 <= r <= n. Then k + n + 1 + floor((k-1)/n) = (q+1)*(n+1) + r.
%C A398590 The column index r is unchanged, while the row index increases from q to q+1 when passing from row length n to n+1.
%C A398590 The sequence is not monotone; for example, a(38) = 240 > 237 = a(39).
%H A398590 Frédéric D. W. Heidenthal-König, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.19121230">Euclidean Transport Update for the Multiplication Table</a>, Zenodo, 2026.
%H A398590 Frédéric D. W. Heidenthal-König, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.20130521">Motion and Animation in Multiplication</a>, Zenodo, 2026.
%H A398590 Frédéric D. W. Heidenthal-König, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.19102497">The Support of the Euclidean Decomposition of the Multiplication Table</a>, Zenodo, 2026.
%F A398590 Let P_n = {i*j: 1 <= i, j <= n}.
%F A398590 a(n) = #{k in P_n: k + n + 1 + floor((k-1)/n) in P_{n+1}}.
%F A398590 a(n) = #{(q, r): 0 <= q < n, 1 <= r <= n, q*n + r in P_n and (q+1)*(n+1) + r in P_{n+1}}.
%F A398590 a(n) = Sum_{k = 1..n^2} A394016(n,k) * A394016(n+1, k+n+1+floor((k-1)/n)).
%e A398590 The distinct entries of the 4 X 4 multiplication table are 1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 8, 9, 12, 16. Under the correspondence (q,r) -> (q+1,r), their respective images are 1 -> 6, 2 -> 7, 3 -> 8, 4 -> 9, 6 -> 12, 8 -> 14, 9 -> 16, 12 -> 19, 16 -> 24.
%e A398590 Among these images, 6, 8, 9, 12, 16 occur in the 5 X 5 multiplication table. They correspond to k = 1, 3, 4, 6, 9. Hence a(4) = 5.
%o A398590 (Python)
%o A398590 def products(n):
%o A398590     return {i * j for i in range(1, n + 1) for j in range(1, n + 1)}
%o A398590 def a(n):
%o A398590     p = products(n)
%o A398590     p1 = products(n + 1)
%o A398590     return sum(k + n + 1 + (k - 1) // n in p1 for k in p)
%o A398590 n = 60
%o A398590 print([a(k) for k in range(1, n + 1)])
%Y A398590 Cf. A027424, A394016.
